THE WAGES OF WAR

The time was when all men fought in one war;
And many a man's hand grew hard by smiting stone;
Many a man with the battle-cry on his lip
Woke to his work at dawn and sang as he went home;
A little mad from much endurance and strife,
But with an elation that made music of life.
Then the old wars ceased: now we fight far away,
And see no more than a narrow red haze;
We feel it only through our pulses beat,
Our souls are sick

The wind was low and slow; it crept upon my ear
Like a sleepy lizard on a stone in sun;
And like a spider's web the air hung near
O'er meadow and marsh-land and the wide blue sea;
There was no wind there--only the light breeze
That skims along shore and off the water's breast.
